Watch Item: HED-Family Pairing within Q1 2026 Fireball Activity

Status
Open watch item
Scope: composition-only, tracked separately from directional/fireball-rate baseline

1. Observed Facts
Q1 2026 shows elevated upper-tail fireball activity (large, widely reported events), not a clear increase in total counts. AMS data indicate ~2,000+ events, with large fireballs (50+ reports) roughly doubled vs. recent averages; many produced sonic booms.
Key events:
March 8, 2026: Western Europe (Koblenz-Güls, Germany) fireball — bright, multi-second duration, visible across multiple countries, sonic booms reported, fragments recovered after roof penetration. Classified as brecciated eucrite (HED family).
March 17, 2026: Ohio (near Valley City/Medina County) daytime fireball — ~2m/7-ton object, sonic booms felt across NE Ohio and beyond, fragments recovered. Confirmed eucrite (HED family).
Two distinct radiant concentrations noted in broader Q1 data: Anthelion zone and high-declination zone. The two HED events have ~98.2° angular separation (unrelated orbits/streams). 33/40 large events associated with sonic booms in analyses.
Historical baseline (2015–2025 Meteoritical Bulletin valid falls): ~116 classified falls, with HED-classified falls at ~6% (7 out of total in that window per prior framing; HED achondrites remain uncommon overall).

2. Baseline Explanation (Directional)
The working baseline for Q1 2026 elevated large fireballs:
Directional geometry (Anthelion + high-declination radiants) increases the rate of slow, deeper-penetrating, recoverable events.
This accounts for more large/widely witnessed fireballs and higher recovery potential. It does not explain compositional clustering (e.g., HED pairing).

